item.description:
The reason you struggle is that you are trying to understand it through thought. That is impossible. The knowing arises from below thought, bubbles up into thought, and voilà the understand ‘enlightens’.

I find the trick is to trust thought only to the degree that it bubble up from ‘below’ and into my awareness spontaneously. It seems we can’t help but over-think life life way too much. The result is life feel ‘complicated’. Chapter 70 seems to speak to this a little…

This resonates.  Thanks Carl.  I&#039;m going to go &quot;bubble.&quot;

The point is that you have no choice. You will &quot;strive on diligently&quot; as best you can no matter what you think. Your natural base is so much more than your &#039;idea&#039;. The mind is like the tip of the iceberg. The bulk (body &amp; emotion) lie below the surface. In some respects, when it tilts left, the mind &#039;thinks&#039; right; when it tilts right, the mind &#039;thinks&#039; left.
The reason you struggle is that you are trying to understand it through thought. That is impossible. The knowing arises from below thought, bubbles up into thought, and voilà the understand &#039;enlightens&#039;.
I find the trick is to trust thought only to the degree that it bubble up from &#039;below&#039; and into my awareness spontaneously. It seems we can&#039;t help but over-think life life way too much. The result is life feel &#039;complicated&#039;. Chapter 70 seems to speak to this a little...
&lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-70/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot;&gt;Our words are very easy to know, very easy to do. Under heaven none can know, none can do. Speech has its faction, involvement has its sovereign. Man only is without knowing, and because of this I don&#039;t know. Knowing self is rare, following self is noble. Because of this, the sacred person wears coarse cloth and cherishes pure.&lt;/a&gt;
Now Mike, go sleep on it, dream, and who knows what tidbit will bubble up into true understanding. That is how it happens for me anyway. Letting go allows the world enter. Of course, you have to hold on for a while, wrestle with it, before you have enough &#039;stuff&#039; to let go of. I&#039;m just providing some stuff to wrestle with. Chapter 36 speaks a little to this...
&lt;a href=&quot;https://centertao.local/tao-te-ching/carl/chapter-36/&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot;&gt;In desiring to inhale through the nose, one must first open up. In desiring a little less, one must first make an effort. In desiring to let go, one must first begin. In desiring to take, one must first give. This saying is little understood. The weak gets the better of the unyielding&lt;/a&gt;
